.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
Educational implementation of the Unix script command (C98 standard) 


.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
pulgamecanica (arosado-)
.RE
.PP
This header defines the core data structures and function interfaces for ft_script, which creates a typescript of terminal sessions using pseudo-terminals (PTY)\&.
.PP
STANDARD: C98 (ISO/IEC 9899:1990) PLATFORM: Linux
.SH "ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW:"
.PP
The script command works by creating a pseudo-terminal (PTY) which consists of two endpoints:
.PP
.IP "1." 4
MASTER side: Controlled by the parent process (our ft_script program)
.IP "2." 4
SLAVE side: Used by the child process (the shell or command being run)
.PP
.PP
The PTY acts like a bidirectional pipe but with terminal semantics, it handles line discipline, terminal control codes, and signals\&.
.SH "PROCESS STRUCTURE:"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
User Terminal
.br
 |
.br
 | (stdin/stdout)
.br
 v
.br
 [Parent Process]
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
Reads from user terminal stdin
.IP "\(bu" 2
Writes to PTY master (sends to child)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Reads from PTY master (receives from child)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Writes to user terminal stdout
.IP "\(bu" 2
Logs output to typescript file
.br
 |
.br
 | (PTY master/slave)
.br
 v
.br
 [Child Process]
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
stdin/stdout/stderr connected to PTY slave
.IP "\(bu" 2
Runs shell or specified command
.IP "\(bu" 2
Behaves as if connected to a real terminal
.PP
.SH "KEY SYSTEM CALLS USED:"
.PP
PTY Creation:
.IP "\(bu" 2
posix_openpt(3) or open(2) with /dev/ptmx - Creates PTY master (we use open('/dev/ptmx', O_RDWR | O_NOCTTY) directly, man 7 pty)
.IP "\(bu" 2
grantpt(3) - Changes ownership of slave to calling user (skipped: on modern Linux with devpts, ownership is set automatically)
.IP "\(bu" 2
unlockpt(3) - Unlocks the slave PTY (we use ioctl(master_fd, TIOCSPTLCK, &unlock) directly, man 2 ioctl_tty)
.IP "\(bu" 2
ptsname(3) - Gets the name of the slave PTY device (we use ioctl(master_fd, TIOCGPTN, &pty_num) and build '/dev/pts/N' manually)
.PP
.PP
Process Management:
.IP "\(bu" 2
fork(2) - Creates child process
.IP "\(bu" 2
setsid(2) - Creates new session (child becomes session leader)
.IP "\(bu" 2
execve(2) - Replaces child process with shell
.PP
.PP
Terminal Control:
.IP "\(bu" 2
tcgetattr(2) - Gets terminal attributes
.IP "\(bu" 2
tcsetattr(2) - Sets terminal attributes
.IP "\(bu" 2
ioctl(2) - Controls terminal device (TIOCSWINSZ for window size)
.PP
.PP
I/O Multiplexing:
.IP "\(bu" 2
select(2) - Waits for multiple file descriptors to become ready
.PP
.PP
Signal Handling:
.IP "\(bu" 2
signal(2) or sigaction(2) - Sets up signal handlers
.IP "\(bu" 2
SIGCHLD - Notifies when child process terminates
.IP "\(bu" 2
SIGWINCH - Notifies when terminal window size changes 
.PP

.SH "Macro Definition Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "#define BUFFER_SIZE   8192"
Buffer size for I/O operations (8KB is a good balance)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Too small: excessive system calls
.IP "\(bu" 2
Too large: increased latency, memory usage 
.PP

.SS "#define DEFAULT_SHELL   '/bin/sh'"
Default shell when SHELL environment variable is not set 
.SS "#define DEFAULT_TYPESCRIPT   'typescript'"
Default output file when no filename is specified 
.SS "#define EXIT_STATUS_SIGNAL_BASE   128"
Exit status when child process is terminated by signal Follows bash convention: 128 + signal_number 
.SH "Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "pid_t check_child_status (pid_t child_pid, int * status)"
Waits for child process and gets exit status
.PP
Uses waitpid() with WNOHANG to check for child termination without blocking\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIchild_pid\fP Process ID of child 
.br
\fIstatus\fP Output parameter for exit status
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Child PID if exited, 0 if still running, -1 on error
.RE
.PP
WNOHANG: Don't block if child hasn't exited yet
.PP
EINTR: waitpid() can be interrupted by signals If EINTR occurs, we retry the wait, this is important because signals can interrupt waitpid() 
.SS "int copy_window_size (int master_fd)"
Copies window size from stdin to PTY master
.PP
This ensures the child process has the correct terminal dimensions\&. Uses TIOCGWINSZ to get stdin's window size and TIOCSWINSZ to set the PTY's window size\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fImaster_fd\fP PTY master file descriptor
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, -1 on error
.RE
.PP
Notes: Sets PTY window size via ioctl 
.SS "int create_pty_master (void)"
Creates and opens a pseudo-terminal (PTY) master
.PP
Uses Linux-specific PTY ioctls directly (no POSIX wrappers):
.IP "1." 4
open('/dev/ptmx', O_RDWR | O_NOCTTY) - opens PTY master (man 7 pty)
.br
 skips posix_openpt(3): equivalent, but avoids the glibc wrapper
.IP "2." 4
ioctl(master_fd, TIOCSPTLCK, &unlock) - unlocks slave (man 2 ioctl_tty)
.br
 skips grantpt(3): not needed on modern Linux devpts (kernel handles it)
.br
 skips unlockpt(3): replaced by the TIOCSPTLCK ioctl directly
.PP
.PP
O_NOCTTY: Don't make this our controlling terminal
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
File descriptor of PTY master on success, -1 on error
.RE
.PP
Ownership: Caller must close returned file descriptor
.PP
Prints error message to stderr on failure 
.SS "pid_t fork_child (int master_fd, const char * slave_name, const \fBscript_options\fP * options)"
Forks and sets up child process to run in PTY slave
.PP
Child process (pid == 0):
.IP "1." 4
setsid(), creates new session, child becomes session leader with no controlling terminal yet (required so that opening the slave makes it the controlling terminal automatically) (man 7 credentials)
.IP "2." 4
open(slave_name, O_RDWR) - opens PTY slave; because the child is now a session leader without a controlling terminal, the kernel assigns it as the controlling terminal for this session
.IP "3." 4
dup2(slave_fd, 0/1/2) - redirects stdin, stdout, stderr to the slave
.IP "4." 4
close(slave_fd) and close(master_fd) - both extra fds are no longer needed
.IP "5." 4
execute_shell() - exec the shell; does not return on success
.PP
.PP
Parent process (pid > 0):
.IP "\(bu" 2
Returns child PID immediately; never touches slave_fd
.PP
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fImaster_fd\fP PTY master file descriptor 
.br
\fIslave_name\fP Path to PTY slave device (e\&.g\&., '/dev/pts/0') 
.br
\fIoptions\fP Script options (determines what command to run)
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Child PID in parent, does not return in child (calls execve) Returns -1 on fork failure
.RE
.PP
Precondition: master_fd is valid PTY master, slave_name is non-NULL 
.SS "char * ft_strcpy (char * dst, const char * src)"
Copies src into dst including the null terminator
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdst\fP Destination buffer (must be large enough for src) 
.br
\fIsrc\fP Null-terminated source string 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
dst
.RE
.PP
Copies src into dst, including the null terminator\&. Returns dst\&. 
.SS "char * ft_uitoa (char * buf, unsigned int n)"
Converts an unsigned integer to its decimal string representation
.PP
Writes the result into buf and null-terminates it\&. Only supports non-negative values (unsigned int)\&. buf must hold at least 11 bytes (10 digits + null) to cover the full range of unsigned int (0 \&.\&. 4294967295)\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIbuf\fP Destination buffer 
.br
\fIn\fP Value to convert 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
buf
.RE
.PP
Writes the decimal representation of n into buf and null-terminates it\&. Supports unsigned int only (no negative numbers, no sign character)\&. Returns buf\&. 
.SS "int get_exit_code (int status)"
Converts child wait status to exit code
.PP
Follows bash convention:
.IP "\(bu" 2
Normal exit: return child's exit code
.IP "\(bu" 2
Signal termination: return 128 + signal_number
.PP
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIstatus\fP Raw status from wait()
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Exit code to return from main() 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const char * get_pty_slave_name (int master_fd)"
Gets the pathname of the PTY slave device
.PP
Uses ioctl(master_fd, TIOCGPTN, &pty_num) to get the slave index (man 2 ioctl_tty), then manually builds the path '/dev/pts/N', skips ptsname(3)\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fImaster_fd\fP File descriptor of PTY master 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Slave device path (e\&.g\&., '/dev/pts/0') on success, NULL on error
.RE
.PP
Ownership: Returned pointer is to static storage, do NOT free
.PP
Notes: Prints error message to stderr on failure
.PP
The slave name is a device path like '/dev/pts/0' or '/dev/pts/1'\&. The child process will open this device file to get access to the slave end of the PTY\&.
.PP
On modern Linux systems, PTY slaves are in /dev/pts/ (pts = pseudo- terminal slave)\&. Older systems used /dev/ptyXX and /dev/ttyXX\&. 
.SS "const char * get_user_shell (void)"
Gets the user's shell from SHELL environment variable
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Shell path, or DEFAULT_SHELL if SHELL is not set
.RE
.PP
Ownership: Returns pointer to environment string, do NOT free 
.SS "void init_options (\fBscript_options\fP * options)"
Initializes \fBscript_options\fP with default values
.PP
Call this before \fBparse_arguments()\fP to ensure all fields have valid defaults\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIoptions\fP Options structure to initialize (must be non-NULL) 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int io_loop (\fBscript_state\fP * state, const \fBscript_options\fP * options)"
Main I/O loop that copies data between terminal and PTY
.PP
This is the heart of the script command\&. It uses select() to multiplex between stdin and the PTY master:
.PP
.IP "1." 4
stdin -> PTY master: User input goes to child (shell)
.IP "2." 4
PTY master -> stdout: Shell output goes to user
.IP "3." 4
PTY master -> output_fd: Shell output also logged to file
.PP
.PP
The loop continues until:
.IP "\(bu" 2
Child process exits (detected by read returning 0 or SIGCHLD)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Error occurs on stdin or master_fd
.PP
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIstate\fP Script state (contains master_fd, output_fd, child_pid) 
.br
\fIoptions\fP Script options (determines flush behavior)
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, -1 on error
.RE
.PP
Precondition: PTY master is open, output file is open, child is running Postcondition: All I/O is complete, child has exited
.PP
Diagram: 
.PP
.nf
stdin -----> PTY master -----> Child (shell)
                 |
                 v
         +-------+-------+
         |               |
         v               v
      stdout      typescript file

.fi
.PP
 Notes:
.IP "\(bu" 2
Reads from stdin and master_fd
.IP "\(bu" 2
Writes to stdout, master_fd, and output_fd
.IP "\(bu" 2
May call fflush() if flush_mode is enabled 
.PP

.SS "int open_output_file (const char * filename, int append_mode)"
Opens the typescript output file
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIfilename\fP Output filename (or NULL for default) 
.br
\fIappend_mode\fP 0 to truncate, 1 to append
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
File descriptor on success, -1 on error
.RE
.PP
Ownership: Caller must close returned file descriptor
.PP
Notes: Creates or truncates file, prints error on failure 
.SS "int parse_arguments (int argc, char * argv[], \fBscript_options\fP * options)"
Parses command-line arguments and populates options structure
.PP
Uses getopt(3) to parse command-line options in a standard way\&. This function follows the POSIX conventions for option parsing\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIargc\fP Argument count from main() 
.br
\fIargv\fP Argument vector from main() 
.br
\fIoptions\fP Output parameter for parsed options (must be non-NULL)
.RE
.PP
Precondition: options must point to valid \fBscript_options\fP structure Postcondition: options is populated with parsed values
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, -1 on error (invalid option or missing argument)
.RE
.PP
Notes: Prints error messages to stderr on parse errors 
.SS "void print_usage (const char * program_name)"
Prints usage information to stderr
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIprogram_name\fP Name of the program (usually argv[0]) 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void restore_terminal_mode (const \fBscript_state\fP * state)"
Restores terminal to original state (before raw mode)
.PP
Should be called before program exit to avoid leaving terminal in an unusable state\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIstate\fP Script state (contains original termios)
.RE
.PP
Precondition: \fBsetup_terminal_raw_mode()\fP was called successfully 
.SS "int setup_signal_handlers (\fBscript_state\fP * state)"
Sets up signal handlers for the session
.PP
Handles:
.IP "\(bu" 2
SIGWINCH: Window size change (terminal resize)
.PP
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIstate\fP Script state (needed by handlers)
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, -1 on error
.RE
.PP
Notes: Installs signal handlers 
.SS "int setup_terminal_raw_mode (\fBscript_state\fP * state)"
Puts terminal into raw mode for character-by-character I/O
.PP
Raw mode disables:
.IP "\(bu" 2
Line buffering (ICANON)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Echo (ECHO)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Signal generation (ISIG)
.IP "\(bu" 2
CR/NL translation (ICRNL, ONLCR)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Flow control (IXON)
.PP
.PP
This allows immediate character forwarding without waiting for newline\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIstate\fP Script state (stores original termios for restoration)
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, -1 on error
.RE
.PP
Precondition: stdin is a terminal (checked with isatty) Postcondition: state->original_termios contains saved attributes state->terminal_modified is set to 1
.PP
Notes: Modifies stdin terminal attributes 
.SS "int write_all (int fd, const char * data, ssize_t len, const char * error_msg)"
Write all data to file descriptor with EINTR retry
.PP
Helper function to ensure all data is written, handling partial writes and EINTR interruptions\&. Used throughout the codebase for reliable writes\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIfd\fP File descriptor to write to 
.br
\fIdata\fP Data buffer to write 
.br
\fIlen\fP Length of data to write 
.br
\fIerror_msg\fP Error message prefix for perror on failure 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, -1 on error 
.RE
.PP

